Title: CLIMATE ANTHOLOGY: SEGMENT II - WILDFIRES
Credit: Written by
Author: Screenwriting Class Collective
Draft date: October 2026
Format: Fountain

/* =========================================================================
   SEGMENT II: WILDFIRES
   Setting: Wildland-Urban Interface (Boreal forest edge / Mountain valley corridor)
   Themes: Extreme Vapor Pressure Deficit, pyrocumulonimbus formation, transboundary smoke.
   Atmospheric Context: 39°C, 9% Relative Humidity, Wind gusts 55 km/h, PM2.5 > 350 µg/m³.
   ========================================================================= */

EXT. PINE RIDGE CREST - LATE AFTERNOON

The sun is not yellow. It is an ominous, blinding MAGENTA DISK hanging in a sickly bronze sky.

A forest of lodgepole pine, desiccated by three consecutive rainless summers, stands brittle. Pine needles crunch like dried glass underfoot.

ELENA (34), in fire-resistant Nomex pants and a dust-coated tactical radio harness, points a laser pyrometer at the tree canopy.

The digital readout FLICKERS: **CANOPY FUEL MOISTURE: 6.2%**.

ELENA
(into radio)
Dispatch, Lookout Seven. Fuel moisture is below six point five. The canopy is essentially kiln-dried timber. If a spark catches the understory, it's going straight to a crown fire.

RADIO DISPATCH (V.O.)
(static-laced)
Copy Lookout Seven. Red Flag Warning extended through zero-eight-hundred tomorrow. We have dry lightning cells building over the south ridge.

Behind Elena, a massive, towering cloud column rises over the jagged ridgeline.

It is not a rain cloud.

It is a PYROCUMULONIMBUS: an erupting volcanic pillar of soot, ash, and boiling amber gases climbing 40,000 feet into the stratosphere.

A sudden DOWNBURST OF HOT WIND slams against Elena, ripping dry pine cones across the dirt road.

EXT. SUBURBAN HIGHLAND ROAD - CONTINUOUS

Ash falls from the sky like soft grey snow, collecting in the wipers of a slow-moving convoy of pickup trucks and family SUVs.

MARCUS (42) frantically cinches tie-down straps over cardboard boxes in the bed of his truck. He wears an N95 respirator; the white fabric is already smudged charcoal grey around his nostrils.

His daughter, LILY (8), sits in the passenger seat, hugging a cloth pet carrier.

LILY
(through the cracked window)
Dad, the streetlights are turning on!

Marcus looks at his watch: **2:15 PM**. 

The sky has turned so dark from the smoke shroud that automatic daylight sensors across the neighborhood have tripped. Streetlights glow an eerie amber through the haze.

MARCUS
Just keep the mask on, Lily. Keep the recirculate button pushed in the car.

A deep CRACKLE, like a freight train rolling through dry brush, echoes from the tree line just 400 yards away. The crowning blaze has breached the ridge.

[[WRITING PROMPT FOR CLASSMATES: Continue Elena's emergency tactical retreat as Marcus navigates the single-lane evacuation route blocked by fallen power poles and abandoned vehicles.]]
